<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">David Friedrich Strauss</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1808">1808</a>–<a href="/years/1874">1874</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Friedrich Strauss was a German theologian and writer from Ludwigsburg, a pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-christian-baur">Ferdinand Christian Baur</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>. His Life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> (1835–1836) treated the gospel miracles as myths shaped by early Christian belief, caused a sensation and cost him his academic career. It became a founding work of the historical study of Jesus and was translated into English by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-eliot">George Eliot</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david-friedrich-strauss.jpg" alt="David Friedrich Strauss" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hermann Günther · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_Friedrich_Strauss_BW.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
